  "version": 190,
  "released": "2026-08-22",
  "changelog": "v190: feat: entity identity context — Play graduates, the Interaction establishes identity (docs/pr-specs/entity-identity-context.md; ADR 0022 amended, ADR 0018 gains a third-instance row; platform ADR 0020 + review-loop/57). Pressing Play on an entity candidate is no longer a read-only research session: it graduates the entity in the background — one roster mutation, one commit — and the conversation that opens establishes WHO or WHAT this is. The first thing you see is one short, natural line chosen for the entity's type (entity_candidate.opening_question — \"Tell me about Ada — who are they to you?\" for a person, \"…what makes it worth keeping?\" for an object). Your first answer gets an ordinary reply that receives what you said, adds exactly one sentence — \"I've added **Ada** as a person in your story — tell me if that's the wrong name or the wrong person.\" — and then asks at most ONE identity question: when the roster already holds a likely-same page, whether this is that same one (that question outranks every other); otherwise, for a person, how they're related to you or whether they're still living. If your answer already said, it asks nothing. After that first reply identity is settled and never raised again; it changes only when YOU raise a change. The conversation may OFFER to start a focus — at most once per session and only for a person, place, period or theme — but it never creates one: a yes is recorded and handed to the focus mechanics you already have. The structured turn output gains exactly one additive field, entity_setup: {aliases?, relationship?, living?, type?, maps_to?, start_focus?} | null, parsed structurally (conversation_delivery._parse_entity_setup — absent or malformed always degrades to null, never an error) and validated against closed vocabularies (entity_candidate.validate_entity_setup; type against entity_roster.ENTITY_TYPES, relationship against focus_candidate.FOCUS_RELATIONSHIPS, maps_to against the caller's own roster slugs so a merge target is never invented). No new session field, lifecycle status, or model purpose — the stage is read straight from the transcript (entity_candidate.entity_stage_for_session), and the duplicate list comes from the roster's OWN matchers (entity_candidate.possible_duplicates reuses entity_roster._entity_keys and focus_dupes._token_subset_pairs; there is no second entity matcher). Seven new entity_setup_gates.* lints (entity_candidate.lint_entity_setup_reply) enforce the aside's shape, silence on every later turn, at most one question, at most one focus offer, and no narrated mechanism; eight new goldens pin all of it and score into the same check_gates call as the existing research_gates.*. And the roster can finally be told what you said: entity-verdict <type> <slug> graduate|never|clear now takes --alias (repeatable), --relationship, --living/--not-living and --maps-to, applying graduation and identity in ONE idempotent call. --maps-to wins over graduate — a mapped entity already has a home — and when it names another entity on the same roster the loser's names fold into the survivor's aliases, which is how this system has always expressed \"this is really that page\". relationship and living survive a roster refresh exactly as keywords and owner verdicts do. The entity → focus hand-off is one new verb and nothing else: focus-recommend-from-entity <type> <slug> appends ONE pending recommendation row (recommend_focuses.recommendation_for_entity, reason \"owner asked during entity onboarding\") and creates no Focus. Every one of these is optional and defaults to off: with no entity_stage the turn appendix is byte-identical to v189, and a three-argument entity-verdict call behaves exactly as it did. The research-mode assets (action/next_gap/dimension_evidence, the seven-dimension rubric, entity-candidate-prompt / entity-candidate-complete and their research_gates.*) are marked superseded for the Play path and otherwise unchanged; their structured-output contract moved out of the prompt leaf into entity_candidate._research_output_contract_block() so the leaf can be replayed verbatim on top of an ordinary Conversation prompt without two output contracts fighting.",
